  "worked_solution": "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false because $3∤(-5-11).$ Notice that $-5-11=-16,$ which is not divisible by $3.$\n\n- Statement II is true because $7\\,|\\,(-7-0).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(-7-0)÷7=-7÷7=-7\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false because $9∤(6-25).$ Notice that $6-25=-19,$ which is not divisible by $9.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
